v 3
d usepackage: find, verify, and install the user-written packages (and data) a do-file needs
d
d usepackage takes the list of user-written packages a do-file needs and makes
d sure they are installed, so a shared do-file can open with one line instead of
d a stack of ssc install / net install commands or a paragraph telling the reader
d to go run findit.  For each name it checks whether it is already installed,
d then SSC, then the net search catalogue (which covers the Stata Journal and
d the STB), reporting what it found, what it skipped, and why.
d
d The name you ask for is often a COMMAND rather than a PACKAGE -- dropmiss ships
d inside dm89_2, bacon inside st0197.  usepackage resolves that case, but because
d the mapping is inferred it asks before installing, and in a batch run it
d declines rather than guessing (see noconfirm).
d
d Ancillary files (a package's example do-files and datasets, which net install
d does not place on the adopath) are fetched by default and reported; noancillary
d skips them.
d
d github() installs a package straight from a GitHub repository, probing branch
d (main, then master) and the usual layouts for stata.toc; give it a bare owner
d and it searches that account's repositories by name, or set the global
d usepackage_github once and any unresolved name is looked for there.  It uses
d only raw.githubusercontent.com for installs, so it is not rate-limited the way
d the GitHub code-search API is.  data() fetches data
d files out of a GitHub repository, guessing the live branch and transparently
d resolving Git LFS pointers, which the raw endpoint otherwise serves as a
d 130-byte text stub that Stata cannot read.  scan() reads a do-file and reports
d which of its commands do not resolve on this machine.
d
d usepackage does not check versions; pair it with require (Correia and Seay,
d Stata Journal 24(4)) when you need minimum or exact versions pinned.
d
d Requires: Stata 16.0 or newer
d
d Author: Eric A. Booth, Sr Researcher, Texas 2036
d Support: eric.a.booth@gmail.com
d
d Version 1.0.0 (2011) was written at the Public Policy Research Institute,
d Texas A&M University.
d
d KW: user-written packages
